{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_20-21-32-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json\np='draft/update.json'\nd=json.load(open(p))\nd['rationale']=(\\\"Both halves of the multi-voter question now have complete comparisons. Receipt abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407 (exit 0, twenty-six events, no parse loss) resolves the election check: with RestoreCommittedLogs on and the restarted node isolated together with one committed voter immediately after NewRaft returns, it became leader with reachable_votes 2 against a committed quorum of 3 (comparison 0 violated, witness event index 22, leader_below_committed_quorum true, majority_side_leader true in the same run), while the flag-off control never won (comparison 1 holds at evaluated event index 25). Together with receipt b15f4b6c55b44402ace926204de9b826 for the configuration loss, the child question is now answered: the stale rebuilt configuration both misdescribes membership and decides an election the committed configuration would refuse. The remaining open item is entry-level divergence, which the registered gap still asks about and which is not claimed. The two config re-checks in this last declaration were redundant and again incomplete because the two multi-voter scenarios emit the same restart event name; they duplicate the already-complete configuration comparison and carry no new claim, so the interpretation rests on the election comparison from this receipt and the configuration comparison from the earlier one.\\\")\nd['sources']=[\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"api_restore_committed_call\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"api.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":604,\\\"end_line\\\":620,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"api_restore_committed_body\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"api.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":724,\\\"end_line\\\":754,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"api_addvoter\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"api.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":994,\\\"end_line\\\":1005,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"raft_requestvote_leader_guard\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":1690,\\\"end_line\\\":1700,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"raft_quorum_size\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":1087,\\\"end_line\\\":1094,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"raft_electself_vote_count\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":1999,\\\"end_line\\\":2060,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}\n]\nd.pop('experiment',None)\nd['knowledge']=[\n {\\\"ref\\\":\\\"@minority_leader_behavior\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n  \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A node restarted with Config.RestoreCommittedLogs whose rebuilt configuration is a stale, smaller membership can be elected leader with support that is a minority of the committed configuration: isolated together with one committed voter it became leader with two votes while the committed five-voter configuration requires three, and the same restart without the flag could not win on that side.\\\",\n  \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Committed configuration grown from three to five voters after the node's snapshot; RestoreCommittedLogs=true with a CommitTrackingLogStore; the restarted node isolated together with one committed voter immediately after NewRaft returns, with that partner also cut off from the majority side so requestVote's existing-leader guard does not refuse it; observed with a bounded window against a flag-off control.\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"api_restore_committed_body\\\",\\\"api_addvoter\\\",\\\"raft_requestvote_leader_guard\\\",\\\"raft_quorum_size\\\",\\\"raft_electself_vote_count\\\"],\n  \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407\\\":[21,22,24,25]}}\n]\nd['interpretations']=[{\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"9eb7105d2f8e48dfa54fcc262ad69cd9-question-0\\\",\n \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"c34a830a404a4dad8ea4e2fe492f5e66-experiment\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"api_restore_committed_call\\\",\\\"api_restore_committed_body\\\",\\\"api_addvoter\\\",\\\"raft_requestvote_leader_guard\\\",\\\"raft_quorum_size\\\",\\\"raft_electself_vote_count\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407\\\":[21,22,24,25],\\\"b15f4b6c55b44402ace926204de9b826\\\":[15,18]},\n \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Two completed comparisons answer this question, each with its flag-off control. Configuration: receipt b15f4b6c55b44402ace926204de9b826, comparison 0 violated at witness event index 15 - the node restarted from a log whose latest configuration is index 14 with five servers came back at configuration index 1 with three servers, the snapshot's bootstrap configuration - with the control holding at evaluated event index 18 (index 14, five servers). Election: receipt abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407, comparison 0 violated at witness event index 22 - the restarted node, isolated together with one committed voter immediately after NewRaft returned, became leader with reachable_votes 2 against committed_quorum 3, and the same run also reports majority_side_leader true, so a leader elected on a minority of the committed configuration coexisted with the majority-side leader - with the control holding at evaluated event index 25 (became_leader false). Source: after restoreFromCommittedLogs (api.go:724-754) advances lastApplied to the persisted commit index, the startup scan at api.go:604-620 starts above the committed configuration entry, so AddVoter's later entry (api.go:994-1005) is never re-read and the node keeps the snapshot's three-server list; quorumSize() (raft.go:1087-1094) then returns two and electSelf (raft.go:1999-2060) asks only that list, so the node's own vote plus one follower meets its stale quorum while the committed configuration needs three. The consequence is safety relevant rather than merely a stale read: a client that applies through this leader receives success for an entry that lacks a majority of the committed configuration. The manouevre the run needed - cutting the single committed voter off from the majority side as well, so requestVote's existing-leader guard (raft.go:1690-1700) does not refuse the vote - is an ordinary partition shape, and two executions produced the outcome while two settled-schedule executions did not, so the manifestation is schedule dependent while the decision itself is unrepaired by the library.\\\",\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\\\"The flag-off controls in both receipts keep the committed configuration and cannot win on the isolated side.\\\",\\\"Two settled-schedule executions did not produce the minority win; the difference is explained by whether the isolated partner still heard the majority-side leader, so no frequency claim is made.\\\",\\\"The redundant configuration re-checks in the last receipt were incomplete because the two scenarios emit the same restart event name; they carry no new claim and are not used.\\\"],\n \\\"limitations\\\":[\\\"Only the leadership decision and the coexistence with the majority-side leader are observed; no entry-level divergence or lost acknowledged entry is witnessed.\\\",\\\"The observed windows are bounded, so no occurrence-rate or absence bound is established.\\\",\\\"The scenario restarts the former leader of an enlarged cluster; other roles, change types and chain orders are not explored.\\\"],\n \\\"answers\\\":{\\\"7da03e78394f41fdad540ae0824ec2a6-gap-1\\\":{\\\"explanation\\\":\\\"The election part is now resolved by a complete comparison: receipt abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407 has comparison 0 violated at witness event index 22 (leader with two votes against a committed quorum of three, co-existing with the majority-side leader) and its control holding at index 25. Reproduction depends on whether the single committed voter on the isolated side still hears the majority-side leader, because requestVote refuses candidates while a leader is known (raft.go:1690-1700); with immediate isolation it reproduced twice, with the settled schedule it did not. Entry-level divergence between the two leaders remains unwitnessed, so the gap stays open on that point.\\\",\\\"resolved\\\":False}},\n \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Receipt b15f4b6c55b44402ace926204de9b826 supplies the configuration comparison and receipt abfdbd1823b342409ef4a815bade0407 supplies the election comparison; both are complete executions of the same five-voter scenario over identical stores, sizes and snapshot timing, and both report the same configuration values (index 1 with three servers against expected 14 with five, controls at 14 with five). They are separate complete histories rather than fragments: each built its own cluster, and only the comparison declarations and result event names differ between them.\\\"}]\njson.dump(d,open(p,'w'),indent=2)\nprint('ok')\nPYEOF\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_20-21-32-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
